# The server's front Caddy (docs/edge.md). One block per hostname the server
# serves, forwarding to the project's own Caddy by the alias it has on the
# `edge` network (its docker-compose.prod.yml). Caddy gets each hostname's
# certificate on its own and redirects HTTP to HTTPS. The aliases end in
# -app or -site, never the bare slug: a server is often named for its slug,
# and that name resolves to the server itself, so a proxy to it would loop.
#
# The projects' Caddyfiles carry the security headers, caching and every
# other rule; this only routes. It trusts no forwarded address: Caddy sends
# each project the visitor's own in X-Forwarded-For, throwing away any the
# visitor sent, and X-Forwarded-Proto: https.
#
# lb_try_duration: a deploy recreates a project's Caddy, and for those few
# seconds a request waits for it rather than failing.
#
# Changing it, on the server, as root (cp it into place, never install or
# mv: docs/edge.md, "Changing it"):
#   cd /opt/edge && docker compose exec caddy caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile
#   docker compose exec caddy caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile
#
# Comments are whole lines only; tests/Unit/EdgeTest.php strips them.

# The app (this repository). A site beside it on the same server (Waffle
# Site's setups 2, 2a and 4) adds its own blocks: docs/edge.md, "A site
# beside the app".
waffle.example.com {
	reverse_proxy waffle-app:80 {
		lb_try_duration 15s
	}
}
